minor type="fixed" "iOS: remote audio no longer has to drop to the device's call volume as soon as local capture starts. iOS applies a reduced, call-tuned output gain while the audio session runs a chat mode (`videoChat`/`voiceChat`), which Apple's Voice Processing I/O compensates for; without VPIO there is nothing to compensate, so a capture that resolves to WebRTC software voice processing now gets `playAndRecord` with the `default` mode and keeps media loudness. The new `AudioManager.setPlatformVoiceProcessingAllowed` guarantees Voice Processing I/O is never used, which is what lets the `default` mode stick — iOS rewrites the mode back to `voiceChat` whenever VPIO is instantiated, so a session mode alone cannot avoid the call-tuned gain. In automatic mode `playAndRecord` is additionally kept across mute toggles until the engine stops, instead of flipping back to `playback` on every mute: that flip moved playback between iOS's media and call volume registers (audible as a loud-then-quiet jump) and tore down echo cancellation mid-call. `LiveKitClient.initialize(bypassVoiceProcessing: true)` now also tells the audio device module to drop processing, matching what it already does to the `getUserMedia` constraints; previously it left Apple voice processing requested, so the session kept a chat mode whose call-tuned gain had nothing left to compensate it and remote audio became close to inaudible. The automatic speaker policy now also sets `defaultToSpeaker`, which the `default` mode needs to stay on the speaker"
